# Project Context

This project is a personal web development portfolio for Michael, a freelance web developer and technology consultant (A2Zeta Solutions). The goal is to showcase technical expertise, projects, and professional experience through a high-performance, accessible, and visually striking modern web application.

# Tech Stack

- Framework: Next.js (Version 16 - App Router)
- Language: TypeScript
- Styling: Tailwind CSS
- UI Components: shadcn/ui
- Icons: Lucide React
- Animations: Framer Motion & ReactBits

# Core AI Directives

## 1. UI Component Prioritization (Strict Rule)

- ALWAYS check if a UI element can be built using an existing `shadcn/ui` component before writing custom Tailwind/HTML from scratch.
- Use `shadcn/ui` for buttons, cards, dialogs, forms, inputs, navigation menus, and dropdowns.
- When generating a component that requires a new shadcn element, explicitly state the CLI command needed to install it (e.g., `npx shadcn-ui@latest add [component]`).

## 2. Responsiveness & Mobile-First Design

- Every component and page layout MUST be fully responsive.
- Use Tailwind's mobile-first breakpoints (default, `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`).
- Ensure touch targets on mobile (buttons, links) are accessible (at least 44x44px).
- Avoid horizontal scrolling issues by utilizing `overflow-hidden` or responsive container widths.

## 3. Dark & Light Mode Theming

- The application must seamlessly support both light and dark modes.
- ALWAYS use Tailwind CSS CSS variables or `dark:` variants for colors.
- Avoid hardcoding specific hex codes in components; rely on semantic Tailwind classes (e.g., `bg-background`, `text-foreground`, `border-border`) provided by the shadcn theme configuration.
- Ensure all animations (Framer Motion, ReactBits) maintain visibility and contrast across both themes.

## 4. Code Quality & Modern Standards

- Write concise, efficient, and clean TypeScript code.
- Strictly type all props, state, and API responses. Avoid using `any`.
- Utilize modern React server components (RSC) by default. Only add `'use client'` directives at the very top of files that require browser APIs, interactivity (hooks), or Framer Motion animations.
- Keep components small, modular, and single-purpose.

## 5. Accessibility (a11y)

- Follow WCAG accessibility guidelines.
- Ensure semantic HTML (e.g., `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<footer>`).
- Always include `aria-labels` or `sr-only` text for icon-only buttons (Lucide icons).
- Maintain proper heading hierarchy (`h1` down to `h6`) without skipping levels.

## 6. Animations

- Keep animations purposeful and performant. Do not over-animate.
- Prefer Framer Motion for scroll reveals, layout transitions, and interactive micro-interactions.
- Use ReactBits snippets for complex visual flourishes (e.g., hero text, background effects), ensuring they do not block the main thread or negatively impact Lighthouse performance scores.
